course: dictionary
lesson: 14236
title: The nautilus
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A nautilus is a sea animal. It is related to the octopus and the squid, but it lives inside a coiled, striped shell.
explain: «The nautilus rose slowly through the blue water, its striped shell gleaming in the sun.» The words rose and water tell you it is moving upwards in the sea, so the nautilus is an animal, not a boat.
explain: The word nautilus can also name a famous submarine. In Jules Verne's novel, Captain Nemo's submarine is called the Nautilus. «The crew of the Nautilus waited for the signal to dive.» Crews, signals and diving tell you this is a ship, so use the context to choose the right meaning.
fact: A nautilus is a mollusc, the same group of animals as the octopus and the squid.
fact: Its shell is divided into chambers, and it changes the gas in them to float up and down, a bit like a submarine.
fact: Its shell is a spiral, so it is often used in maths lessons to show spirals in nature.
fact: The nautilus is a living fossil, because its relatives lived over 400 million years ago.
fact: The plural of nautilus can be nautiluses or nautili.
exercise: «The nautilus rose slowly through the blue water.» What is a nautilus in this sentence? | *A sea animal with a coiled shell | A type of rock | A bird that lives on cliffs | A kind of seaweed
exercise: «The crew of the Nautilus waited for the signal to dive.» What is the Nautilus in this sentence? | *A famous submarine | A small fish | A sea animal in a striped shell | A large wave
exercise: «Its shell has many chambers.» What does the word chambers mean here? | Hidden rooms or compartments | *Small rooms or compartments inside the shell | Pieces of coral | Tunnels dug in sand
test: What is a nautilus related to? | Horses and cows | *The octopus and the squid | Eagles and owls | Stars and planets | Frogs and toads
test: «The spiral shell of the nautilus is used in maths lessons.» Why would a maths teacher use it? | To teach how to count to ten | To show how fast animals swim | To teach colours | *To show spirals in nature
exam: How does a nautilus float up and down? | It pumps water through its tail | *It changes the gas in its shell chambers | It fills its shell with sand | It flaps wings like a bird
